diagnosis_text:
A thorough five-layer investigation found no active fault anywhere in the Kath4 topology. The reported symptom — users on two segments (VLAN5 and VLAN6) unable to reach their gateways — cannot be confirmed. Every gateway across all five VLANs responds with 0% packet loss: VLAN5 (10.5.0.254 on sshswitch br_v5), VLAN6 (10.6.0.254 on sshswitch br_v6), VLAN10 (172.16.10.254 on mswitch0 vlan10), VLAN20 (172.16.20.254), and VLAN30 (172.16.30.254). All 28 host-to-host reachability pairs pass, OSPF is fully converged on all nine routers (17 routes each, all adjacencies Full), no traffic-shaping or ACL drops are present, and no resource contention is detected.

The most likely explanation for the complaint is a past transient event that coincided with a topology reconfiguration: switch4 now uplinks to sshswitch (ms1_sw4) rather than to mswitch0 (leaving mswitch0.eth5/link_msw0_sw4 dangling). That change is complete and functional — sshswitch correctly acts as the Layer-3 routing switch for VLAN5 and VLAN6, and OSPF propagates those prefixes to the rest of the fabric. Because no flap script, carrier-change counter anomaly, or daemon log entry supports an ongoing fault, the gateway-unreachability claim is unconfirmed as a present-state defect.

Root cause: No fault exists in the current network state; the complaint is unconfirmed and likely reflects a transient condition during the switch4 uplink change that has since self-resolved.

Fix: No corrective action required. If connectivity complaints recur, re-check whether any hosts still hold a stale ARP or static route pointing at a gateway IP from the old mswitch0 path.
=== END ===